import json
from pathlib import Path

s=json.loads(Path('summary.json').read_text());tr=''
for r in s:
 if r['omega']!=.1:continue
 f=lambda x:f'{100*(x-1):+.3f}%'
 a,b=r['localRatio'],r['remoteRatio'];tr+=f'<tr><th>{r["family"]} · {r["n"]**2} · {r["pattern"]}</th><td>{f(a["mean"])} [{f(a["min"])}, {f(a["max"])}]</td><td>{f(b["mean"])} [{f(b["min"])}, {f(b["max"])}]</td></tr>'
table='<table><caption>Ω=0.1: damaged / intact − 1. Mean and range of five seed means; each seed averages x/y drive.</caption><thead><tr><th>Architecture · nodes · links</th><th>Local compliance change</th><th>Remote RMS change</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+tr+'</tbody></table>';Path('table.html').write_text(table)
html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Larger-network microrheology before and after damage</title><style>body{font:17px system-ui;background:#f7f5ed;color:#203b32;max-width:1200px;margin:25px auto;padding:20px}p{line-height:1.6}img,canvas{width:100%;height:auto}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}th,td{padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd4c7;text-align:left}button,select{font:inherit;padding:10px}input{width:35%}.table{overflow:auto}a{color:#275f4c}</style><h1>How does local collagen damage change local and remote response?</h1><p>40 specimens: 100 and 196 nodes, disordered and modular, distributed and clustered links, five seeds. Intact/damaged × x/y probe direction × six dimensionless frequencies gives 960 harmonic systems; 958 pass. Two static responses are flagged because one damaged modular specimen has a free zero-stiffness motion.</p><p>A four-node virtual probe drives the network at (0.35L,0.50L). The same three collagen fibers nearest (0.55L,0.50L) are cut in each placement pair. All outer nodes are clamped. Added-link count is 67 or 131, keeping count per node approximately fixed. The lesion is near the central cluster, so this does not test arbitrary damage sites.</p><h2>Before and after: frequency response</h2><img src="local-compliance.png" alt="Local oscillatory compliance before and after damage across two sizes and architectures"><img src="remote-response.png" alt="Remote response before and after damage across two sizes and architectures"><div class="table">'''+table+'''</div><p>Remote RMS uses free nodes at least 0.6 from the drive centroid (three reference mesh spacings). Receiver sets are identical within each intact/damaged pair and vary between sizes. Seed ranges are descriptive, not confidence intervals. Static exclusions do not affect the Ω=0.1 table.</p><h2>Inspect the harmonic displacement fields</h2><label>View <select id="mode"><option value="total">Intact and damaged displacement</option><option value="difference">Damage-induced change</option></select></label> <button id="play">Play</button><label> Phase <input id="phase" type="range" min="0" max="360" value="72"></label><label> Vector magnification <input id="gain" type="range" min="1" max="8" value="1"></label><p id="status" aria-live="polite"></p><canvas id="view" width="1200" height="1000" aria-label="Four harmonic displacement-field comparisons"></canvas><p>Red dashed: cut fibers. Pale purple: added links. Orange: probe nodes. Gray vectors: intact; green: damaged, or damaged minus intact in the difference view. One fixed vector scale across panels within each view; changing magnification applies to all panels. End dots mark vector tips. Linear phasors are reconstructed at each phase; no new damage occurs during oscillation.</p><h2>Publication animations</h2><p><a href="oscillatory-response.gif">Download intact/damaged GIF</a> · <a href="damage-induced-response.gif">Download damage-induced-change GIF</a></p><img src="damage-induced-response.gif" alt="Animated effect of three cut fibers on the remote oscillatory displacement field"><h2>Interpretation and limits</h2><p>For the selected central lesion, clustered links reduce the change in local and remote response relative to distributed links. This supports protection by constraints near this lesion, not a universal ranking of crosslink patterns or biological age. The two sizes remain different and do not establish boundary convergence or a long-range decay law.</p><p>The model assumes uniform nodal drag and a linear response about an unprestressed elastic state. There is no explicit solvent flow, rigid bead, optical trap, thermal noise or measured time scale. Ω is not hertz and these are not calibrated tissue storage/loss moduli. Probe amplitudes were checked against the nonlinear gradient at two small displacements.</p><p><a href="METHODS.md">Full methods and caveats</a> · <a href="paired.csv">Paired response changes CSV</a> · <a href="results.json">All responses, fields and checks</a> · <a href="validation.json">Validation</a> · <a href="summary.json">Summary values</a> · <a href="../force-robustness/">Previous force/damage robustness study</a> · <a href="https://humanavianaisymbiosis.wordpress.com/2026/10/31/collagen-aging-topology-crosslink-placement/">WordPress post</a></p><script src="animation-data.js"></script><script src="ui.js"></script></html>''';Path('index.html').write_text(html)
